cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Keyword Frames Are Cluster Samples: How a Third of an npm Ecosystem
  Stayed Invisible
message: If you use this dataset or these results, please cite it as below.
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Poddar
    given-names: Prachet
    email: prachetpoddar@gmail.com
    affiliation: University of California, Los Angeles
version: 3.4.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22641812
date-released: 2026-09-08
license: Apache-2.0
license-url: https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0
abstract: >-
  A measurement study of Model Context Protocol servers on npm that became a
  paper about why measuring them kept failing. A keyword-built frame samples
  publishers rather than packages, because npm's search API is a top-slice and
  keyword tagging is a publisher-level habit; the population size moves by a
  factor of 8.6 depending on which keyword defines it; and a third of the
  enumerated population is machine-generated by four scopes. The dependency
  resolver used throughout had four silent defects, found only by comparing
  against a real npm install, and each changed a published number. Nothing
  measured shows elevated supply-chain risk on any dimension a registry
  exposes. The release documents every withdrawn figure across all versions,
  including several that had no artifact behind them.
keywords:
  - software supply chain
  - Model Context Protocol
  - SBOM
  - null results
  - measurement study
  - sampling bias
repository-code: https://github.com/prachetpoddar/mcp-supply-chain-study
identifiers:
